[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one parser bug, one fix queued. The reports in it
describe three problems. Each problem has its own ticket below, with its own count. The fix
queued for the parser closes only the first one.

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} />

<Callout kind="warn">
Shipping the parser fix does not close #4471. The <code>hint</code> and <code>one-day</code>
reports keep coming after it ships, because their causes are elsewhere.
</Callout>

## The three problems

**`parser`: the shared parser reads every date month first.** Our tenants write the day first,
so any date whose day is 12 or less comes out with day and month swapped: 7 April becomes
4 July. This is the bug #4471 names. Its fix is the one queued.

**`hint`: booking-v3 prints the wrong format under its date field.** Tenants on `booking-v3`
show `MM/DD/YYYY` under the date field. Their people type what the hint says, month first, and
the form reads it the tenant's way, day first. The dates swap the same way, but the parser fix
does not touch them. Only reports sent through the form of a day-first tenant on booking-v3
belong here. The fix is the hint (or the form), not the parser.

**`one-day`: dates move by one day, not by a swap.** These reports have no day/month swap in
them. A Tuesday shows as the Monday before it, a whole month is "one day off", one client gets
Tuesday and another gets Monday for the same meeting, an invite arrives before the booking was
made. That pattern points at time zones (a date stored or rendered at midnight in the wrong
zone), not at date order. The desk saw it and wrote these ids down in `desk-notes.json`, but
[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) still counts them under `parser`.

<ProblemReports ticket={4471} problem="one-day" />

## How the reports are placed

1. **Count each report once.** The export repeats some reports (pages overlap); one id is one
   report.
2. **Place by tenant, not by intake tag.** `meta.intakeTag` is a guess from the reporter's
   browser. The cause depends on the tenant's locale and booking form, and on the channel
   (`causeOf` in `causes.ts`). Intake's tags disagree with that for dozens of reports, in both
   directions; the table shows the split.
3. **A one-day move is its own problem.** A report listed in `desk-notes.json` as a one-day move
   goes to `one-day` before the tenant rule runs (`problemOf` in `problems.ts`).

Every number on this page is computed from `reports-4471.json` when the page renders. When the
desk merges more reports into #4471, the counts here move with them.

## Why one ticket hid three

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject alone. All three problems
produce the same subjects ("Wrong day on the invite", "Month and day swapped?"), so the subject
rule cannot tell them apart. The page for #4471 already computes two causes; its own table flags
`hint` as "not this ticket — no ticket of its own":

